    kref_get(&io->refcnt);
    io->done = &wait;
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the `io` object is referenced (via `kref_get(&io->refcnt)`) in the asynchronous I/O case when it is also marked as blocking. However, in non-blocking cases, this additional reference is not needed, leading to a situation where the `io` object can be freed while still being accessed, resulting in a use-after-free error.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
bool blocking = io->blocking;
if (!blocking)
```
Reason:
The fix involves storing the blocking state in a separate variable (`blocking`) before the call to `fuse_aio_complete()`. This ensures that the original `io->blocking` value is preserved and used for the conditional check, preventing the use-after-free issue by avoiding unnecessary references to the `io` object after it may have been freed.

